IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate

-- THREE-VALUED HELPERS for snap1's modules. They carry no provision, so their names begin with the part's own prefix
-- `snap1` (no other part uses it). A MAYBE BOOLEAN is JUST TRUE (the text says yes), JUST FALSE (the text says no in
-- its own words) or NOTHING (the text does not say). The operators are Kleene's: a JUST FALSE decides an AND whatever
-- the other side is, a JUST TRUE decides an OR, and anything else that is not decided stays NOTHING.

GIVEN a IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      b IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`snap1 both` MEANS
    CONSIDER a
        WHEN NOTHING THEN
            CONSIDER b
                WHEN JUST y THEN IF y THEN NOTHING ELSE JUST FALSE
                WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        WHEN JUST x THEN
            CONSIDER b
                WHEN JUST y THEN JUST (x AND y)
                WHEN NOTHING THEN IF x THEN NOTHING ELSE JUST FALSE

GIVEN a IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      b IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`snap1 either` MEANS
    CONSIDER a
        WHEN NOTHING THEN
            CONSIDER b
                WHEN JUST y THEN IF y THEN JUST TRUE ELSE NOTHING
                WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        WHEN JUST x THEN
            CONSIDER b
                WHEN JUST y THEN JUST (x OR y)
                WHEN NOTHING THEN IF x THEN JUST TRUE ELSE NOTHING

-- A plain BOOLEAN fact, lifted: the caller stated it, so it is known.
GIVEN a IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`snap1 known` MEANS JUST a

-- Three-valued NOT: the text's yes is a no and the text's no is a yes; NOTHING stays NOTHING.
GIVEN a IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`snap1 not` MEANS
    CONSIDER a
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        WHEN JUST x THEN JUST (NOT x)

-- Has the text said yes? Used inside a rule to ask "is this limb definitely met" without ever answering FALSE for NOTHING.
GIVEN a IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`snap1 is definitely yes` MEANS
    CONSIDER a
        WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
        WHEN JUST x THEN x

-- Has the text said no?
GIVEN a IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`snap1 is definitely no` MEANS
    CONSIDER a
        WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
        WHEN JUST x THEN NOT x

-- WHOLE MONTHS from one date to a later one, counted by calendar day: 15 March to 14 March of the next year is 11, to 15 March is 12.
-- A period "of 12 months from" a date therefore still runs on the day before the anniversary. A date that is before the first is not covered here:
-- the callers test the order of the two dates themselves. A month-end start (31 January to 28 February) counts 0, because the day of the month is smaller.
GIVEN earlier IS A DATE
      later IS A DATE
GIVETH A NUMBER
`snap1 whole months from the earlier date to the later date` MEANS
    ((DATE_YEAR later MINUS DATE_YEAR earlier) TIMES 12 PLUS (DATE_MONTH later MINUS DATE_MONTH earlier)) MINUS (IF DATE_DAY later LESS THAN DATE_DAY earlier THEN 1 ELSE 0)
